WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.808
Spotify keeps who-played-what in SQL.

00:00:05.808 --> 00:00:09.616
But a song's lyrics or audio file is a blob.

00:00:09.616 --> 00:00:13.424
Cramming it into SQL columns is like stuffing a video into a spreadsheet cell.

00:00:13.424 --> 00:00:16.768
So a blob goes in a key-value store.

00:00:16.768 --> 00:00:20.111
The simplest database there is.

00:00:20.111 --> 00:00:23.455
Give it a key, get a value back.

00:00:23.455 --> 00:00:26.798
No schema, no joins, no constraints.

00:00:26.798 --> 00:00:30.142
You trade SQL's rich queries for one thing done fast: lookup by key.

00:00:30.142 --> 00:00:33.374
Mickey's twenty most recent plays.

00:00:33.374 --> 00:00:36.606
In SQL, without the right index, you scan and sort a shared table.

00:00:36.606 --> 00:00:39.838
In a wide-column store?

00:00:39.838 --> 00:00:43.069
Just one partition.

00:00:43.069 --> 00:00:46.301
Key by user, sort by time, and the rows sit in order already.

00:00:46.301 --> 00:00:49.533
Read the first twenty, stop.

00:00:49.533 --> 00:00:52.765
Modeling the access pattern into the key is the whole game.

00:00:52.765 --> 00:00:56.558
So key-value trades query power for raw speed on the lookups that matter, when you model the key around the read.

00:00:56.558 --> 00:01:00.350
Next, the cost of getting any of this data wrong.

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:04.143
Security.
